course: dictionary
lesson: 12975
title: Thorough and complete
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Exhaustive means complete and thorough, so nothing is left out. Imagine searching every drawer in the house for a lost key, not just the first one you open.
explain: «We made an exhaustive list of every pet shop in town.» The word list is a clue. An exhaustive list has every item on it, not just a few of the best ones.
explain: In maths, an exhaustive search tries every possible case, one after another, until none are left. That is how you can be sure you have found all the answers.
fact: Exhaustive means complete and thorough, covering every part or every possibility.
fact: «An exhaustive search» means you have checked every place or every possible answer.
fact: In maths, an exhaustive list shows every possible case, such as all the ways to make 3p using 1p and 2p coins.
fact: «The scientist's exhaustive research left nothing out.»
fact: Do not mix up exhaustive with exhausting, which means very tiring.
exercise: «Mia made an exhaustive list of every pet shop in town.» What does exhaustive mean here? | *Complete, with nothing left out | Short and quick | Very noisy | Bright and shiny
exercise: «The police began an exhaustive search of the park.» What does the search do? | It stops after five minutes | It looks only at the pond | *It checks every part of the park | It skips the trees
exercise: Which list of ways to make 3p is exhaustive? | Only 2p and 1p | Only 1p, 1p and 1p | *2p and 1p, and 1p, 1p and 1p: all the ways | Just one coin of 3p
test: «Our exhaustive research included every book in the library.» What does exhaustive mean? | Missing some parts | Quiet | Very tired | *Complete and thorough
test: Which word is closest in meaning to exhaustive? | *Thorough | Tiny | Careless | Sleepy
exam: «An exhaustive study of the castle left out nothing, from the cellars to the towers.» What does exhaustive mean in this sentence? | Half-finished | *Complete, covering every part | Very old | Careless and fast
